# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# InnoVision — PRODUCTION RUNTIME secrets template.
#
# This is the SOURCE for `deploy/secrets/prod.env.enc`. To change production
# secrets you decrypt, edit, and re-encrypt — never edit the .enc by hand:
#
#   # first time only (generates the age key + creates the .enc):
#   bash deploy/secrets/bootstrap.sh
#   # every time after:
#   sops deploy/secrets/prod.env.enc
#
# On the VPS this decrypts to `.env.local` (mode 0600) at deploy time and is
# read by the compose `app` service via `env_file:`.
#
# ── WHAT IS *NOT* IN THIS FILE, AND WHY ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. The BUILD-time family — `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`,
#    `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL`, `SITE_URL`,
#    `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.
#
#    These are INLINED by `next build` on a GitHub runner, and the VPS pulls the
#    finished image from GHCR — so nothing set here can change them. Verified in
#    this repo: `.next/standalone` contains the literal Supabase URL and does
#    NOT contain the string `process.env.NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`. Putting them
#    in this file would be worse than useless: it would LOOK like the deployment
#    target, and editing it would change nothing while appearing to. They live
#    as GitHub Actions secrets/vars instead, and `deploy/remote-deploy.sh`
#    verifies the running image actually baked what CI intended.
#
#    (Two of them — NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL and the anon key — ARE echoed at
#    the bottom of this file, commented out, for the operator scripts that run
#    ON the VPS: `scripts/vps-smoke.mjs` reads them from `.env.local` to locate
#    the Supabase project for its login round trip. They are read-only
#    conveniences for those scripts and are NOT read by the app.)
#
# 2. `PROD_ENV_STRICT` — pinned to "1" by docker-compose.yml's `environment:`
#    block. Compose `environment:` beats `env_file:`, so setting it here would
#    be a no-op that implies control you do not have. Do NOT set it in a
#    developer's .env.local either: a bare `node server.js` with it set refuses
#    to boot (that is the point — see src/lib/prod-guards.ts).
#
# 3. `INSIGHTFACE_BASE_URL` — compose overrides it to
#    `http://insightface-service:8000` (the compose-network service name). The
#    app's own default is `http://localhost:8000`, which inside the app's
#    container resolves to the app itself, where nothing is listening.
#
# 4. `GLM_BASE_URL` / `VLLM_API_KEY` — the LOCAL OCR leg's variables. This host
#    runs `GLM_PROVIDER=remote`, so they are inert. Leaving them unset is
#    correct and load-bearing: `VLLM_API_KEY` is never reused as a Z.ai key
#    (GLM_OCR_SETUP.md §6), and setting them would suggest a local vLLM that
#    this GPU-less host does not have.
#
# 5. `PORT` / `HOSTNAME` / `NODE_ENV` — the image sets them (3000 / 0.0.0.0 /
#    production). The container listens on 0.0.0.0 INSIDE the container; the
#    host-side publish is loopback-only (see docker-compose.yml).
#
# 6. The MIGRATION credentials — `SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD`,
#    `SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF`. Schema is pushed to the hosted project by the
#    `migrate` job in .github/workflows/deploy.yml, running on a CI runner, so
#    those live in GitHub secrets/variables and never reach the VPS.
#
#    This is a security boundary, not a convenience. Those credentials can
#    ALTER and DROP every table in the project; the app needs neither (it talks
#    to Supabase over HTTPS/PostgREST with the service-role key below). Putting
#    schema-mutating credentials on the internet-facing host to save a CI job
#    would widen the blast radius of a host compromise from "read the data" to
#    "rewrite the schema". See docs/DEPLOY_CICD.md §4.5.
#
# ── THE KILL SWITCHES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `E2E_RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_E2E_FAKE_SEAM`, `FACE_MOCK_ENABLED`,
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_INTEGRITY_HARDENING_OFF` must stay UNSET or "0". They are listed
# at the bottom, commented out, as a checklist. Under `PROD_ENV_STRICT=1` the
# app REFUSES TO BOOT if one is armed — the failure is loud, not silent.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═══ Supabase (hosted project) ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
# Server-only. NEVER a NEXT_PUBLIC_* value and never a build arg — this key
# bypasses RLS entirely.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ═══ Origin / proxy posture ══════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The ONE origin var read PER REQUEST (`checkSameOrigin` in src/lib/http.ts).
# Must be the full scheme-included public origin, exactly as the browser sends
# it in `Origin:`. A missing scheme or a trailing slash makes every server
# action from that origin 403 while pages still render — a genuinely confusing
# failure, so copy it from the address bar.
TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://innovision.example.edu

# Trusted proxy hops in front of the app, counted from the RIGHT of
# X-Forwarded-For. Set by the deployment topology, not by preference:
#   1 = host nginx only            ← THIS deployment (deploy/nginx/innovision.conf)
#   2 = Cloudflare/tunnel + nginx
#   0 = no proxy (direct)          ← MANDATORY if anything reaches :3000 directly
# A wrong value here does not error; it silently collapses every per-IP rate
# limit into ONE shared bucket (too high) or lets a client forge its own bucket
# (too low). See the hop-count table in the Caddyfile header for the measured
# behaviour of each chain.
TRUSTED_PROXY_COUNT=1


# ═══ Face sidecar ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Shared secret for the insightface sidecar's /extract. An EMPTY value is a
# prod-guards VIOLATION under PROD_ENV_STRICT=1 (the app refuses to boot), by
# design: the client omits the x-sidecar-token header when it is empty, so an
# unauthenticated /extract is an embedding oracle for anything that can reach
# it. Generate with: openssl rand -hex 32
#
# ⚠️ THIS VALUE MUST MATCH THE CONTAINER'S. The sidecar reads its copy from the
# project-root `.env` (compose interpolation), NOT from this file. The deploy
# script writes/reads that file separately — see docs/DEPLOY_CICD.md §"Sidecar
# token". Setting it here alone leaves the sidecar accepting anything.
FACE_SIDECAR_TOKEN=

# Anti-spoof enforcement (audit-2 C-01). 1 = a spoof verdict fails the check;
# 0 = record-only. Keep 1 in production: the photo/replay gap is the finding.
FACE_SPOOF_ENFORCE=1


# ═══ GLM-OCR — the REMOTE (Z.ai) leg ═════════════════════════════════════════
# `remote` is the ONLY value that selects the metered leg; unset/`local`/a typo
# selects local and emits a one-time server warn. Fail-closed by design so an
# operator typo can never silently start spending (GLM_OCR_SETUP.md §6).
GLM_PROVIDER=remote

# The root ALREADY carries /api/paas/v4 — do NOT append /v1. A trailing /v1
# makes the endpoint 404.
ZAI_BASE_URL=https://api.z.ai/api/paas/v4

# Z.AI Open Platform API key: register → top-up → create.
# ⚠️ This is a METERED, BILLABLE credential. The caps below are the only ceiling
# on what a compromised or abusive account can spend, so do not raise them
# without reading docs/COSTS.md §2.3.
ZAI_API_KEY=

# Same model id on both legs.
OCR_GLM_MODEL=glm-ocr

# Page cap per OCR run. ⚠️ 30 is an INTERIM value: the runbook records a
# 100-vs-30 conflict that live-curl testing has not resolved
# (DEPLOY_VPS.md §4.2). 30 is the conservative arm — raise only after measuring
# tokens/page against the real API.
GLM_REMOTE_MAX_PAGES=30

# Per-user, per-day token ceiling. THE primary spend control.
GLM_DAILY_TOKEN_CAP=2000000

# Cost-logging only (does not enforce anything). Verify against the current
# Z.ai price list before trusting the numbers it prints.
GLM_TOKEN_PRICE_PER_MILLION=0.03

# Probe-cache TTLs. The remote probe is BILLED, so the positive TTL is
# deliberately long; the negative one is short so a transient upstream failure
# is retried promptly rather than hiding the engine for 5 minutes.
GLM_PROBE_TTL_MS=300000
GLM_PROBE_NEGATIVE_TTL_MS=30000

# ⚠️ GLM_SPEND_LEDGER_PATH is NOT set here on purpose: docker-compose.yml pins
# it to /var/lib/innovision/glm-spend-ledger.json, on the `glm-ledger` named
# volume, so the daily cap survives `up --build` / `down`/`up` / image bumps.
# Setting it here would be overridden by compose and would break the volume
# mount's whole purpose. The ledger is PER-HOST — keep this deployment
# SINGLE-INSTANCE (two replicas sharing one Z.ai key double-spend).

# Operator kill switch: 1 refuses EVERY remote call with 503
# `glm_model_unavailable`, without switching legs. Use it to stop spending
# immediately on a suspected key leak. Leave 0.
GLM_SPEND_DISABLED=0


# ═══ OCR engine selection ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# tesseract is the client-side WASM default (free, no network). GLM is the
# server-side engine selected above.
OCR_DEFAULT_ENGINE=tesseract
OCR_VISION_MODEL=deepseek-v4-flash


# ═══ Quiz generation gateway ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
AI_BASE_URL=https://api.kenari.id/v1
AI_API_KEY=
AI_MODEL=


# ═══ AI marking worker (short_text) ══════════════════════════════════════════
# The 0057 `sweep_ai_marks()` cron CLAIMS pending short_text answers and POSTs
# them to AI_MARK_WORKER_URL. That function runs INSIDE Postgres, so it reads
# these from the DATABASE (Vault secret / `app.settings`) — NOT from this file,
# and not from the app container. `deploy/sync-migrations.sh` writes them at
# deploy time from exactly these variables.
#
# Without them the sweep claims rows and never POSTs: every short_text answer
# stays `pending`, which blocks v_all_done, so the quiz never auto-reveals.
# Leaving both blank is safe (the script falls back to SITE_ORIGIN and
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY respectively).
AI_MARK_WORKER_URL=
AI_MARK_WORKER_KEY=


# ═══ Web research (optional) ═════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Absent = the web-topic generation mode is hidden entirely. That is a valid
# posture; the feature fails closed rather than erroring.
TINYFISH_API_KEY=


# ═══ Auth policy ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The single privilege gate to the lecturer role (security audit H-1). An empty
# value DISABLES lecturer self-signup. Use a fresh, high-entropy value (≥ 24
# chars) — never reuse the CI value from .github/workflows/ci.yml, and rotate it
# after any cohort turnover. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
LECTURER_INVITE_CODE=

# ── ROTATION GRACE (audit-2 M-19) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set this to the OLD code while rotating, then delete it after the grace window.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: rotating the code outright breaks every signup already in
# flight. A student who submitted the old code gets an email confirmation, and
# the confirmation lands AFTER the rotation — so the promotion half finds no
# valid code and the signup dies with no error the student can act on. Both
# values are compared in constant time, so carrying the old one for a day costs
# nothing beyond that day's grace.
#
# Rotation procedure:  LECTURER_INVITE_CODE_PREVIOUS=<old>
#                      LECTURER_INVITE_CODE=<new>
#   …wait one day, then remove the PREVIOUS line entirely.
LECTURER_INVITE_CODE_PREVIOUS=

# SSO second trust layer. Comma-separated. Empty = any domain may attempt SSO.
# Set this to your institution's domains to stop a personal-account sign-in.
INSTITUTIONAL_EMAIL_DOMAINS=


# ═══ Optional tunables — sane defaults, set only to change them ══════════════
# These are documented because the env-parity check requires it, NOT because a
# deployment must set them. Leaving them unset uses the default shown, which is
# the value the security audits were written against.
#
# Password-reset budgets (src/lib/auth/reset.ts). The per-IP limits are sized
# ABOVE the per-email ones deliberately: a classroom behind one NAT shares an
# IP, so a tight per-IP budget locks out an entire class while one student
# retries. Do not lower these without reading the audit notes in that file.
# RESET_RATE_LIMIT=5                  # per-email, per minute
# RESET_IP_RATE_LIMIT=30              # per-IP, per minute
# RESET_CONFIRM_RATE_LIMIT=10         # per-IP, per minute (confirm endpoint)
# RESET_EMAIL_DAILY_LIMIT=10          # per-email, per 24h — the DAY bound
#
# Streamed-AI silence tolerance: how long a generation may stall BETWEEN chunks
# before the app gives up (src/lib/ai/client.ts). The total round trip is
# separately capped at 10 minutes (AI_ROUND_TRIP_TIMEOUT_MS).
# AI_STREAM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS=90000


# ═══ Destructive-script confirmation (optional) ══════════════════════════════
# Required by `scripts/lib/remote-env.mjs` for `--remote` DESTRUCTIVE runs
# (db:reset:remote, seeding). ⚠️ Must NOT be the project ref, the Supabase URL,
# or any value readable from .env.production.local — the gate refuses those
# outright. Pick a fresh out-of-band secret.
PROD_CONFIRM_TOKEN=


# ═══ Operator-script conveniences — NOT read by the app ══════════════════════
# `scripts/vps-smoke.mjs` reads these from .env.local to find the Supabase
# project for its login round trip. The IMAGE has its own copies baked in; these
# only serve the scripts that run on the VPS.
#
# ⚠️ If you set them, keep them IDENTICAL to the GitHub build secrets. A
# mismatch does not affect the app at all, but it makes the smoke log in to a
# different project than the app talks to — and its PASS then means nothing.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=


# ═══ MUST STAY UNSET — harness kill switches (gate S5) ═══════════════════════
# Listed so the checklist is in one place. Under PROD_ENV_STRICT=1 the app
# REFUSES TO BOOT with any of these armed. Do not uncomment.
#
# E2E_RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED=1
# FACE_MOCK_ENABLED=1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_E2E_FAKE_SEAM=1
# NEXT_PUBLIC_INTEGRITY_HARDENING_OFF=1
